1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,264
The Padang and the Cricket Club:

2
00:00:02,344 --> 00:00:05,275
The Field That Was Ordered to Stay Empty

3
00:00:05,275 --> 00:00:10,548
In the middle of Singapore's civic
district, in front of the old City Hall,

4
00:00:10,628 --> 00:00:14,625
lies 4.3 hectares of grass
that has never been built on.

5
00:00:14,625 --> 00:00:19,167
Armies have camped on it, kings'
birthdays have been saluted across it,

6
00:00:19,247 --> 00:00:22,227
a surrender was signed
in the chamber above it,

7
00:00:22,307 --> 00:00:26,263
and a nation's first cabinet was
sworn in within sight of it —

8
00:00:26,343 --> 00:00:30,900
but for two hundred years the field
itself has stayed empty, by order.

9
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:36,475
In 2022 the emptiness itself
was made a national monument.

10
00:00:36,475 --> 00:00:39,512
This is the story of
the Padang, and of the

11
00:00:39,592 --> 00:00:43,000
two Victorian sports
clubs that guard its ends.

12
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,303
"Padang" is simply the Malay
word for a field or open plain,

13
00:00:47,383 --> 00:00:50,525
and the plain was old
before Singapore was.

14
00:00:50,525 --> 00:00:54,348
The Sejarah Melayu, the Malay
Annals, calls it "the plain

15
00:00:54,428 --> 00:00:58,046
near the mouth of the river"
at Temasek, and legend in

16
00:00:58,126 --> 00:01:02,085
the same text has Sang Nila
Utama spotting his lion on this

17
00:01:02,165 --> 00:01:06,000
shore in the 13th century —
legend, but the right shore.

18
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,653
When Raffles and William
Farquhar landed at the Singapore

19
00:01:09,733 --> 00:01:13,582
River mouth on the 28th of
January 1819, it was Farquhar who

20
00:01:13,662 --> 00:01:17,446
picked the open plain for the
camp: the ground was cleared,

21
00:01:17,526 --> 00:01:21,572
the Union Jack raised the next
morning, and the treaty with the

22
00:01:21,652 --> 00:01:25,450
Sultan and the Temenggong
followed on the 6th of February.

23
00:01:25,450 --> 00:01:29,675
The field's survival was
decided three years later.

24
00:01:29,675 --> 00:01:33,821
Raffles returned in 1822
to find European merchants

25
00:01:33,901 --> 00:01:38,625
building on land he had ordered
kept for public purposes.

26
00:01:38,625 --> 00:01:41,974
He stopped the building,
and the town plan drawn by

27
00:01:42,054 --> 00:01:45,336
Lieutenant Philip Jackson
under his instructions —

28
00:01:45,416 --> 00:01:49,437
the plan whose authorship this
blog has untangled elsewhere —

29
00:01:49,517 --> 00:01:52,475
marks the field plainly
as an "Open Square".

30
00:01:52,475 --> 00:01:56,451
It was first the Sepoy
Lines and the Cantonment,

31
00:01:56,531 --> 00:02:01,014
then simply the Plain; from
1831 it was the Esplanade,

32
00:02:01,094 --> 00:02:05,150
a name it kept until
"Padang" took over in 1907.

33
00:02:05,150 --> 00:02:06,846
What the square was for,

34
00:02:06,926 --> 00:02:10,625
Singapore decided for
itself: everything outdoors.

35
00:02:10,625 --> 00:02:13,632
An artillery salute for the King's

36
00:02:13,712 --> 00:02:16,800
birthday crossed it in April 1824.

37
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:21,955
From the 1830s and 1840s the New
Year brought a regatta on the water

38
00:02:22,035 --> 00:02:26,652
and sports on the grass —
tug-of-war, foot races, pony races,

39
00:02:26,732 --> 00:02:32,275
sack races, rickshaw races, and, the
record insists, a race after a pig.

40
00:02:32,275 --> 00:02:36,971
By 1845 the crowd needed managing:
fearing for children's safety,

41
00:02:37,051 --> 00:02:41,747
the Municipal Committee ringed
the green with bollards and chains

42
00:02:41,827 --> 00:02:46,750
to protect it "from the incursions
of pony-racing drunken sailors".

43
00:02:46,750 --> 00:02:49,555
At the field's northern end a small knoll,

44
00:02:49,635 --> 00:02:53,058
once mounted with guns
against attack from the sea,

45
00:02:53,138 --> 00:02:57,591
became the place where colonial
society gathered to exchange news,

46
00:02:57,671 --> 00:03:00,625
and was named, with
feeling, Scandal Point.

47
00:03:00,625 --> 00:03:04,554
Fourteen centuries of the
town lie under the grass,

48
00:03:04,634 --> 00:03:09,821
as the 1984 Fort Canning dig and
a 2003 excavation at one corner of

49
00:03:09,901 --> 00:03:14,775
the cricket ground both showed
— but the surface stayed grass.

50
00:03:14,775 --> 00:03:17,525
Cricket came early and stayed.

51
00:03:17,525 --> 00:03:21,388
The game was being played
on the field by 1837 —

52
00:03:21,468 --> 00:03:27,300
the earliest mention is a complaint
about Europeans playing on Sundays.

53
00:03:27,300 --> 00:03:32,928
In October 1852 a meeting formally
established the Singapore Cricket Club;

54
00:03:33,008 --> 00:03:35,627
the first two matches arranged both

55
00:03:35,707 --> 00:03:40,178
failed to raise full sides,
the first eleven-a-side fixture

56
00:03:40,258 --> 00:03:44,500
came in March 1853, and 28
members signed on that year.

57
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:47,283
The club is Singapore's second-oldest,

58
00:03:47,363 --> 00:03:50,075
a decade younger than the Turf Club.

59
00:03:50,075 --> 00:03:54,175
Its pavilion grew with it,
at the field's southern end.

60
00:03:54,175 --> 00:03:58,267
First, in the 1860s, came
a simple wooden hut under

61
00:03:58,347 --> 00:04:02,600
the angsana trees at the
Esplanade's western corner.

62
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:06,540
A single-storey bungalow followed in 1877,

63
00:04:06,620 --> 00:04:09,300
when membership stood at 98.

64
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:14,065
In 1884 the club built on its
present site the pavilion that

65
00:04:14,145 --> 00:04:20,040
still forms the core of today's
clubhouse; membership reached 378 by 1891,

66
00:04:20,120 --> 00:04:24,400
an addition followed in 1907,
and in 1922 the northern

67
00:04:24,480 --> 00:04:29,325
and southern wings completed
the building much as it stands.

68
00:04:29,325 --> 00:04:34,932
Cricket was only the start: an
annual tennis tournament from 1875,

69
00:04:35,012 --> 00:04:40,189
lawn bowls from the 1870s, hockey
from 1892, and football and

70
00:04:40,269 --> 00:04:45,525
rugby planted in Singapore in
the 1880s with the club's help.

71
00:04:45,525 --> 00:04:50,439
Touring sides made the Padang
a stop on the cricket map —

72
00:04:50,519 --> 00:04:54,119
Charles Macartney's Australian XI in 1927,

73
00:04:54,199 --> 00:04:59,551
Sir Julien Cahn's XI in 1937,
the first English team to visit,

74
00:04:59,631 --> 00:05:04,100
and a Commonwealth XI with
Garfield Sobers in 1964.

75
00:05:04,100 --> 00:05:07,468
But the club at the southern
end was, in practice,

76
00:05:07,548 --> 00:05:11,261
for Europeans only — club
membership, as Roots puts it,

77
00:05:11,341 --> 00:05:15,882
was "exclusively for British and
Europeans residents in Singapore",

78
00:05:15,962 --> 00:05:20,100
and it was opened to Asians
only after the Second World War.

79
00:05:20,100 --> 00:05:24,250
So the field acquired a
second club as an answer.

80
00:05:24,250 --> 00:05:28,853
On the 23rd of June 1883 some
thirty Eurasian men founded the

81
00:05:28,933 --> 00:05:33,153
Singapore Recreation Club,
and the government granted it

82
00:05:33,233 --> 00:05:37,836
the Padang's northern end for
its cricket; its first pavilion

83
00:05:37,916 --> 00:05:42,135
was completed there in November
1885, a second clubhouse

84
00:05:42,215 --> 00:05:46,975
opened in 1905, and the present
seven-level clubhouse in 1997.

85
00:05:46,975 --> 00:05:50,686
The SRC's own colour
line lasted eighty years:

86
00:05:50,766 --> 00:05:55,219
only on the 11th of February
1963 did its members vote,

87
00:05:55,299 --> 00:05:59,750
overwhelmingly, to open
membership to all communities.

88
00:05:59,750 --> 00:06:03,577
Two clubs, one field, and
for most of a century a line

89
00:06:03,657 --> 00:06:07,925
drawn down the middle of the
grass that nobody had painted.

90
00:06:07,925 --> 00:06:11,399
The field reached its
present shape around 1890,

91
00:06:11,479 --> 00:06:16,213
when reclamation pushed the
shoreline out and roughly doubled it;

92
00:06:16,293 --> 00:06:21,550
the new road between field and sea
was renamed Connaught Drive in 1907.

93
00:06:21,550 --> 00:06:25,975
What rose behind it turned a
sports ground into a stage.

94
00:06:25,975 --> 00:06:30,266
In 1926 the Municipal Commission
began its new headquarters

95
00:06:30,346 --> 00:06:34,563
on part of the old Hotel de
l'Europe site: designed by the

96
00:06:34,643 --> 00:06:38,193
municipal architect S. D.
Meadows and, from 1925,

97
00:06:38,273 --> 00:06:43,452
his successor Alexander Gordon, the
Municipal Building was completed in

98
00:06:43,532 --> 00:06:47,527
1929 at a cost of about two
million Straits dollars and

99
00:06:47,607 --> 00:06:52,200
opened by Governor Sir Hugh
Clifford on the 23rd of July 1929.

100
00:06:52,200 --> 00:06:56,773
Renamed City Hall in 1951,
when Singapore became a city,

101
00:06:56,853 --> 00:07:00,675
it gave the Padang its
backdrop and its steps.

102
00:07:00,675 --> 00:07:05,131
Beside it in 1939 rose the domed
Supreme Court, on the site of

103
00:07:05,211 --> 00:07:09,887
the Grand Hotel de l'Europe, whose
guests had watched the parades

104
00:07:09,967 --> 00:07:14,650
from its verandahs — that building's
story is told in that post.

105
00:07:14,650 --> 00:07:18,839
The field's most famous piece
of furniture arrived on the

106
00:07:18,919 --> 00:07:23,557
27th of June 1887, when Governor
Frederick Weld unveiled Thomas

107
00:07:23,637 --> 00:07:28,425
Woolner's bronze statue of Raffles
at the centre of the sea side,

108
00:07:28,505 --> 00:07:32,175
the highlight of Queen
Victoria's Golden Jubilee.

109
00:07:32,175 --> 00:07:36,196
It stood in the line of fire:
stray footballs hit it often

110
00:07:36,276 --> 00:07:40,722
enough that in February 1919, for
the centenary of the founding,

111
00:07:40,802 --> 00:07:44,550
it was moved to Empress
Place, where it stands today.

112
00:07:44,550 --> 00:07:48,951
The Padang kept escaping the
planners — a governor's idea of

113
00:07:49,031 --> 00:07:53,133
selling it in the 1830s, a
proposal in the 1850s to move

114
00:07:53,213 --> 00:07:57,614
Saint Andrew's Cathedral into
its centre, and a 1930s scheme

115
00:07:57,694 --> 00:08:02,100
to pave it into a plaza, dropped
after the public objected.

116
00:08:02,100 --> 00:08:06,420
King George V's Silver
Jubilee was celebrated on the

117
00:08:06,500 --> 00:08:10,900
grass in 1935, and then the
stage was struck by war.

118
00:08:10,900 --> 00:08:15,429
On the 17th of February 1942, two
days after the fall of Singapore,

119
00:08:15,509 --> 00:08:20,657
the Japanese used the Padang as an
assembly ground: the European population,

120
00:08:20,737 --> 00:08:24,578
civilians and prisoners of
war, was held and interrogated

121
00:08:24,658 --> 00:08:28,223
on the field before being
marched off to internment —

122
00:08:28,303 --> 00:08:33,325
the civilians to Changi Prison, whose
own story this blog told yesterday.

123
00:08:33,325 --> 00:08:37,710
On the 3rd of March the Eurasian
community was assembled in front

124
00:08:37,790 --> 00:08:42,792
of the Recreation Club's clubhouse,
which served as a registration centre;

125
00:08:42,872 --> 00:08:46,925
those of British or Dutch
parentage were sent to the camps.

126
00:08:46,925 --> 00:08:50,436
The Municipal Building
became the headquarters of

127
00:08:50,516 --> 00:08:55,052
the Syonan Tokubetsu-si, the
Japanese municipal administration,

128
00:08:55,132 --> 00:08:57,550
and the Padang its parade ground.

129
00:08:57,550 --> 00:09:02,478
The Cricket Club's pavilion became
a Japanese restaurant, the Yamoto,

130
00:09:02,558 --> 00:09:07,275
and its clubhouse the Syonan-ko
Tonan Club, an officers' tearoom.

131
00:09:07,275 --> 00:09:12,375
On the 6th of July 1943 Prime
Minister Tojo Hideki and Subhas

132
00:09:12,455 --> 00:09:16,960
Chandra Bose reviewed a
parade of Indian National Army

133
00:09:17,040 --> 00:09:22,050
troops on the field before a
crowd reported at some 25,000.

134
00:09:22,050 --> 00:09:25,867
By 1944, with food scarce,
the Padang had been dug

135
00:09:25,947 --> 00:09:29,451
up and planted with
tapioca — part of the same

136
00:09:29,531 --> 00:09:33,350
Grow More Food drive that
sent settlers to Endau.

137
00:09:33,350 --> 00:09:38,178
In July 1945 Bose laid the foundation
stone of an INA memorial at

138
00:09:38,258 --> 00:09:44,219
the field's southern edge; it was built
that August and demolished within weeks,

139
00:09:44,299 --> 00:09:48,225
on Mountbatten's orders,
after the British returned.

140
00:09:48,225 --> 00:09:51,175
A marker stands on the site today.

141
00:09:51,175 --> 00:09:55,950
The Padang's most retold
moment is usually told wrong.

142
00:09:55,950 --> 00:09:59,070
The Japanese surrender of 1945 was

143
00:09:59,150 --> 00:10:02,350
not signed on the City Hall steps.

144
00:10:02,350 --> 00:10:06,681
It was signed inside, in the
Municipal Building's chamber,

145
00:10:06,761 --> 00:10:10,788
at 11.10 in the morning on
the 12th of September 1945,

146
00:10:10,868 --> 00:10:16,111
when Lord Louis Mountbatten, Supreme
Allied Commander South East Asia,

147
00:10:16,191 --> 00:10:20,450
accepted the surrender of
Japanese forces in the region.

148
00:10:20,450 --> 00:10:24,297
General Seishiro Itagaki signed
the Instrument of Surrender

149
00:10:24,377 --> 00:10:28,889
on behalf of Field Marshal Count
Terauchi, who had suffered a stroke;

150
00:10:28,969 --> 00:10:32,416
eleven copies were signed,
for the British, American,

151
00:10:32,496 --> 00:10:36,942
Chinese, French, Dutch, Australian,
Indian and Japanese governments,

152
00:10:37,022 --> 00:10:40,150
the King, the Supreme
Commander and the record.

153
00:10:40,150 --> 00:10:45,076
Some 400 spectators watched, among
them Malayan community leaders,

154
00:10:45,156 --> 00:10:49,025
the Sultan of Johore and
released prisoners of war.

155
00:10:49,025 --> 00:10:52,655
The steps and the field got the
ceremony around the signing:

156
00:10:52,735 --> 00:10:56,365
four guards of honour, a fleet
band, a seventeen-gun salute,

157
00:10:56,445 --> 00:10:59,952
Mountbatten's address from the
steps, and a victory parade

158
00:11:00,032 --> 00:11:03,105
across the Padang — after
which Japanese prisoners,

159
00:11:03,185 --> 00:11:07,124
never officially called prisoners,
were set to cleaning the city,

160
00:11:07,204 --> 00:11:11,100
the Municipal Building among the
first buildings they scrubbed.

161
00:11:11,100 --> 00:11:14,878
In January 1946 Mountbatten
was back on the field,

162
00:11:14,958 --> 00:11:19,816
decorating sixteen Malayan
resistance leaders led by Chin Peng —

163
00:11:19,896 --> 00:11:24,525
eleven of whom silently answered
with clenched-fist salutes.

164
00:11:24,525 --> 00:11:28,618
In December 1950 the field
saw the other face of civic

165
00:11:28,698 --> 00:11:32,481
life when the Maria Hertogh
riots erupted in front

166
00:11:32,561 --> 00:11:36,425
of the Supreme Court:
eighteen dead in three days.

167
00:11:36,425 --> 00:11:39,575
The chamber then had a second life.

168
00:11:39,575 --> 00:11:44,052
On the 3rd of June 1959 the
People's Action Party held its

169
00:11:44,132 --> 00:11:48,688
victory rally on the Padang
before a crowd Lee Kuan Yew put

170
00:11:48,768 --> 00:11:53,325
at some 50,000, and Lee used
the field itself as his text.

171
00:11:53,325 --> 00:11:57,009
His party had wanted to hold
night rallies there, he said,

172
00:11:57,089 --> 00:12:00,902
but "a small group of Europeans
who were given this field by

173
00:12:00,982 --> 00:12:03,693
the former Colonial
Government, refused it,

174
00:12:03,773 --> 00:12:08,575
although they only use it in the day
time for a few people to play games".

175
00:12:08,575 --> 00:12:13,225
"Well," he concluded, "times have
changed, and will stay changed."

176
00:12:13,225 --> 00:12:17,561
Two days later, on the 5th of June
1959, Lee and his eight ministers

177
00:12:17,641 --> 00:12:22,107
were sworn in inside City Hall — in
the same chamber where Mountbatten

178
00:12:22,187 --> 00:12:26,133
had taken the surrender —
deliberately breaking protocol to be

179
00:12:26,213 --> 00:12:30,224
sworn in there rather than at
Government House, and wearing the

180
00:12:30,304 --> 00:12:34,850
party's open-necked white shirts and
trousers instead of lounge suits.

181
00:12:34,850 --> 00:12:39,085
That December, in the same
building, Yusof Ishak became the

182
00:12:39,165 --> 00:12:43,326
first Malayan-born Yang
di-Pertuan Negara and the flag and

183
00:12:43,406 --> 00:12:47,575
anthem were unveiled, a story
this blog has told in full.

184
00:12:47,575 --> 00:12:51,876
In August 1963 a rally on
the Padang demanding Japanese

185
00:12:51,956 --> 00:12:56,177
war reparations drew a crowd
reported at some 120,000,

186
00:12:56,257 --> 00:13:00,319
among the largest gatherings
in Singapore's history;

187
00:13:00,399 --> 00:13:03,904
a month later, on the
16th of September 1963,

188
00:13:03,984 --> 00:13:09,400
Lee proclaimed Singapore's merger
into Malaysia from the same field.

189
00:13:09,400 --> 00:13:14,373
On the 9th of August 1966, at nine
in the morning, Singapore held its

190
00:13:14,453 --> 00:13:19,207
first National Day Parade on the
Padang: over 23,000 participants,

191
00:13:19,287 --> 00:13:23,967
the Straits Times reported, among
them six People's Defence Force

192
00:13:24,047 --> 00:13:29,100
contingents and four government
ministers marching as officer cadets.

193
00:13:29,100 --> 00:13:33,272
The Padang remained the
parade's only home until

194
00:13:33,352 --> 00:13:36,727
the parades were decentralised in 1975;

195
00:13:36,807 --> 00:13:41,325
the first National Stadium
parade followed in 1976.

196
00:13:41,325 --> 00:13:46,101
The parade that fixed the field
in the national memory was 1968's,

197
00:13:46,181 --> 00:13:50,074
which went ahead in pouring
rain and became the emblem

198
00:13:50,154 --> 00:13:54,200
of the "rugged society" the
young republic meant to be.

199
00:13:54,200 --> 00:13:58,095
Parades moved to the evening from 1973,

200
00:13:58,175 --> 00:14:03,190
and from 1984 the Padang
came back into rotation —

201
00:14:03,270 --> 00:14:08,898
every five years from 1995,
and for the milestone years:

202
00:14:08,978 --> 00:14:10,834
2010, SG50 in 2015,

203
00:14:10,914 --> 00:14:16,825
the bicentennial in 2019, and
2023, 2024 and SG60 in 2025.

204
00:14:16,825 --> 00:14:20,430
The field's modern life
runs on the same double

205
00:14:20,510 --> 00:14:23,725
track as its old one: ceremony and sport.

206
00:14:23,725 --> 00:14:27,540
On the 28th of September
2008 the first Formula 1

207
00:14:27,620 --> 00:14:30,720
night race ran down Saint Andrew's Road,

208
00:14:30,800 --> 00:14:35,650
past the Padang and City Hall,
on its way to Anderson Bridge.

209
00:14:35,650 --> 00:14:39,066
In 2015 the two buildings behind the field

210
00:14:39,146 --> 00:14:42,725
reopened as the National
Gallery Singapore.

211
00:14:42,725 --> 00:14:46,908
And the sports never left: cricket
is still played on the Padang —

212
00:14:46,988 --> 00:14:50,266
the Cricket Club's square
is, Sport Singapore notes,

213
00:14:50,346 --> 00:14:55,110
the only cricket square in Singapore
— the SCC's international rugby sevens

214
00:14:55,190 --> 00:15:00,147
is held on the field, the Recreation
Club runs its weekly soccer and softball,

215
00:15:00,227 --> 00:15:03,650
and the Singapore Marathon
starts and finishes there.

216
00:15:03,650 --> 00:15:09,050
The two clubs now describe themselves
as the field's co-custodians.

217
00:15:09,050 --> 00:15:11,861
What protects it now is the strangest

218
00:15:11,941 --> 00:15:14,675
entry on Singapore's monument list.

219
00:15:14,675 --> 00:15:18,645
The Padang was announced for
gazetting in August 2019,

220
00:15:18,725 --> 00:15:21,045
but the law had to change first:

221
00:15:21,125 --> 00:15:25,920
the Preservation of Monuments Act
was amended in November 2021 to

222
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:29,900
let a monument be an open
site, not only a building.

223
00:15:29,900 --> 00:15:33,208
On the 9th of August 2022, National Day,

224
00:15:33,288 --> 00:15:37,782
the Padang became Singapore's
75th National Monument —

225
00:15:37,862 --> 00:15:41,250
the first green, open space on the list.

226
00:15:41,250 --> 00:15:45,318
The gazette, the Minister for
Culture, Community and Youth said,

227
00:15:45,398 --> 00:15:49,077
"will protect it, as it is
now, from alteration and change

228
00:15:49,157 --> 00:15:52,317
which would affect its
character and significance,

229
00:15:52,397 --> 00:15:56,675
while allowing it to continue to
be a social and community space."

230
00:15:56,675 --> 00:16:00,381
A month later the Padang
and its ring of monuments

231
00:16:00,461 --> 00:16:03,789
were put forward as the
Padang Civic Ensemble

232
00:16:03,869 --> 00:16:07,050
on UNESCO's World Heritage Tentative List.

233
00:16:07,050 --> 00:16:11,231
Every city keeps a field it
cannot bring itself to build on;

234
00:16:11,311 --> 00:16:15,137
Singapore is the one that
wrote the order down in 1822,

235
00:16:15,217 --> 00:16:19,114
watched empires, occupiers
and a new nation all obey it,

236
00:16:19,194 --> 00:16:22,665
and in 2022 made the
emptiness itself a monument —

237
00:16:22,745 --> 00:16:28,000
a lesser-known national treasure
precisely because there is nothing there.
